ALLOCATOR CHURN CANNOT MOVE PEAK HEAP AND DOES MOVE PEAK RSS — ~100 BYTES OF
RESIDENT-NOT-LIVE MEMORY PER ALLOCATION ON macOS, AND ~0 ON LINUX.

date:   2026-09-03
tree:   940b855a (main) = 01ef6f1f + the one-byte `MeCandidate` commit + the
        `perf_encode` one-copy harness commit.
hosts:  x86 = r7900x, x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu (heap via heaptrack, RSS via
        `/usr/bin/time -v`, median of 5 / 3).
        arm = mac, Apple silicon / macOS, 16 KiB pages, libmalloc (RSS via
        `/usr/bin/time -l`, median of 5 / 3). NO HEAP NUMBER — no heaptrack.
cell:   gradient 2048x2048 qp 40, presets 6 and 13, arms and harness as in
        benchmarks/mem_harness_2026-09-03.tsv. Harness subtracted throughout as
        `n_frames * w*h*3/2`, which is exactly what it now is on both sides.
data:   benchmarks/mem_churn_rss_2026-09-03.tsv

=================== WHY THIS FILE EXISTS ======================================

`benchmarks/mem_heaptrack_satd_2026-09-03.meta` concluded, from twelve heaptrack
cells that all read +0.01 MiB after three allocation-site hoists:

  "Removing allocator churn cannot lower a peak: every hoisted buffer is freed
   before the next is taken. The memory gap stays a lifetime property."

**The first sentence is true of PEAK HEAP and false of PEAK RSS**, and peak RSS
is the quantity `tools/mem_gate.sh` and the acceptance criterion use. A buffer
that is freed before the next is taken was never part of the live high-water —
but its PAGES stay resident on an allocator that does not hand them back, and
the next allocation may be satisfied from a fresh region rather than that one.

=================== THE MEASUREMENT ===========================================

Difference the videokey and inter arms, harness removed. That is what ONE INTER
FRAME costs, in three quantities plus the number of allocations it performs:

  preset  quantity                    videokey    inter   MARGINAL
    6     live bytes (x86 heap)         94.14   106.58    +12.44 MB
    6     peak RSS, x86                107.89   124.12    +16.23 MB
    6     peak RSS, arm                131.68   187.07    +55.39 MB
    6     allocation calls           8,225,365 8,679,561   +454,196
   13     live bytes (x86 heap)         76.81   100.15    +23.34 MB
   13     peak RSS, x86                 83.83   105.10    +21.27 MB
   13     peak RSS, arm                 92.21   141.10    +48.89 MB
   13     allocation calls           2,496,391 2,710,505   +214,114

Now subtract the live bytes from the resident bytes and divide by the
allocations that produced them:

  preset   RESIDENT-NOT-LIVE, arm    per allocation
    6      55.39 - 12.44 = 42.95 MB    94.6 bytes
   13      48.89 - 23.34 = 25.55 MB   119.3 bytes

  preset   RESIDENT-NOT-LIVE, x86    per allocation
    6      16.23 - 12.44 =  3.79 MB     8.3 bytes
   13      21.27 - 23.34 = -2.07 MB    -9.7 bytes

**Between preset 13 and preset 6 the inter frame's LIVE cost FALLS by 47 %
(23.34 -> 12.44 MB) while its resident cost on macOS RISES by 13 %
(48.89 -> 55.39 MB) — and its allocation count rises by 112 %.** The excess
does not track live bytes at all; it tracks allocation count, at roughly 100
bytes per allocation, with the two points agreeing to 26 % across a 2.1x change
in count and a 1.9x change in live bytes.

On Linux the same subtraction gives ±10 bytes per allocation — i.e. nothing,
and the sign flips between the two presets, which is what "no effect plus
measurement noise" looks like.

=================== THE CAUSAL READING IS FALSIFIED — SEE §4 =================

The correlation above is measured and stands. **The obvious causal reading of
it — "remove N allocations, get 100*N bytes of macOS RSS back" — was tested
directly and is FALSE.** §4 is that test. Read it before acting on this file.

=================== WHAT FOLLOWS ==============================================

1. ~~Removing N allocations from a frame is worth roughly 100 * N bytes of peak
   RSS on macOS and nothing on Linux.~~ **RETRACTED BY §4.** What survives is
   the correlation and its consequence for METHOD: a peak-heap null is not a
   peak-RSS null, the two quantities move independently, and a memory claim
   must name which one it is about.

2. ~~The three allocation-site hoists of 2026-09-03 should be re-measured on
   macOS RSS, at ~100 bytes each.~~ **WITHDRAWN.** §4 removes 5x as many
   allocations as those three did and measures nothing, so there is no reason
   to expect them to have moved peak RSS either. Their heap NULL stands and
   their CPU win stands.

3. It also explains the ISA split the day's other records report without
   explaining: `benchmarks/mem_aarch64_2026-09-03.meta`'s 36 MB aarch64-over-x86
   gap on the inter arm, its absence on the still arm, and its growth with
   picture size are all "the inter path performs more allocations".

=================== 4. THE DIRECT TEST: A NULL ON macOS, A REGRESSION ON LINUX

`partition::extract_neighbors_tiled` is the port's single largest
ALLOCATION-COUNT site: **637,972 calls on this cell, 23.5 % of the whole
process**, for two `Vec`s of at most 64 bytes each that die before the next
block is predicted, with 128 B of peak heap. If the rate above were causal it
is the biggest single lever available.

It was hoisted into a per-thread scratch (the same shape
`leaf_funnel::detect::txb_coeff_satd` uses), which removes **488,413
allocations on the videokey arm and 499,865 on the inter arm — 18-20 % of every
allocation the process makes.** Peak heap is unchanged to the digit (83.10 M /
112.73 M) and the `.obu` is unchanged, as expected.

Peak RSS on macOS, interleaved A/B, eleven paired rounds per cell, median:

  cell             before    after   after/before
  1536 inter       88512     88080      0.995
  2048 inter      150096    152880      1.019

**NULL on macOS.** Both cells are inside the run-to-run spread, which on this
host is 15 % ([149888, 171312] before, [147872, 168128] after at 2048).

**And WORSE on Linux** — 114,724 -> 118,472 KiB at 2048 inter, `1.033`, over
fifteen paired rounds whose distributions do not overlap ([114392, 115004]
against [118060, 118952]). Removing a fifth of the process's allocations bought
nothing on one host and cost 3.7 MiB on the other. (Peak heap is unchanged on
both, so the Linux rise is resident-page PLACEMENT, not live bytes.)

The change was therefore REVERTED; `benchmarks/neighbor_scratch_ab_2026-09-03.meta`
carries its full four-axis, two-ISA measurement.

So the ~100 bytes/allocation of §2 is a CORRELATION across two presets and not
a lever. The most likely reason it does not transfer: these are 64-byte
allocations served out of libmalloc's tiny/nano magazines, which reuse the same
already-resident pages, so they never grow the resident set at all. Whatever
scales the macOS resident-not-live excess with MD work, it is not the COUNT of
small allocations. A candidate consistent with both results is the SIZE
distribution — transient allocations large enough to take fresh regions — and
that is not measured.

Its CPU delta was a ~0.7 % win on aarch64 and a null on x86; that did not pay
for the Linux memory regression, so it is not in the tree.

=================== NOT ESTABLISHED ===========================================
  * The aarch64 LIVE bytes. The `live_MB` column is x86 heap, used for the arm
    subtraction on the assumption that peak heap is ISA-invariant. The
    allocation SIZES are the same code, so this is likely, but heaptrack does
    not exist on macOS and it is NOT measured. Every "resident-not-live, arm"
    figure inherits that assumption.
  * A LAW. Two presets on one cell is two points. The per-allocation constant
    is a rate estimate, not a fit; 94.6 and 119.3 bytes are not the same number.
  * WHICH allocator behaviour produces it. Region reuse across frames is the
    hypothesis (benchmarks/mem_aarch64_2026-09-03.meta §2b); libmalloc's
    retention POLICY is ruled out there by measurement (the env knobs move the
    cell <= 4 %), a flat per-process ISA tax is ruled out there too (the still
    arm agrees across the ISAs to within 2-5 MB), and **the COUNT of small
    allocations is ruled out by §4**.
  * That removing allocations reduces peak RSS. §4 removed a fifth of them,
    measured nothing on macOS and a 3.3 % REGRESSION on Linux. Do not quote
    §2's bytes-per-allocation as a budget.
  * Any content but gradient, qp but 40, size but 2048x2048, preset but 6/13.
  * Repeat variance on the allocation counts and the heap: one run per cell.
